<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#lettres-de-cachet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lettres de cachet</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as lettre de cachet, lettres de petit cachet, lettres de petit signet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lettres de cachet were letters signed by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, countersigned by one of his ministers and closed with the royal seal, carrying the king's orders, often beyond appeal. Written orders of this kind arose in the 14th century from the principle that the king was released from the laws. The best known were penal: a subject could be imprisoned without trial or defence, confined or exiled, often at a family's request, as with the Marquis de Sade, held from 1777 to 1790, and Mirabeau, whose attack on them was published in 1782. A symbol of the abuses of the Ancien Régime, they were suppressed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> revived their penal equivalent in 1801.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lettres-de-cachet.jpg" alt="Lettres de cachet" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tangopaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lettre_de_cachet_(1703).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French kings could imprison subjects without trial by <b>lettres de cachet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>By a <b>lettre de cachet</b> the king could imprison a subject without trial.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
